# What does a tax destroy?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why does a tax cost more than the money it raises? Why does doubling a tax roughly quadruple the waste?"
next: {"title":"What happens when the government caps rents?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/18-what-happens-when-the-government-caps.cast"}

## Nine windows
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 8 windows
      - 9 windows
      - 10 windows
    values:
      - 3.9
      - 18.4
      - 4.6
    value_labels: true
    bar_colors: same
    icons:
      - house
      - house
      - house
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 25
    y_label: Share of houses (%)
    x_label: Ludlow, 1747–57
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: oates_schwab
      title: 'The Window Tax: A Case Study in Excess Burden'
      authors: Oates, W. E. & Schwab, R. M.
      year: 2015
      finding: In 493 Ludlow dwellings (1747–57 tax records), 18.4% had exactly 9 windows against 3.9% with 8 and 4.6% with 10; spikes also at 14 and 19 windows, just under the tax's notches. For a household at a notch the excess burden was about 62% of tax paid; a 9-window household paid no tax, so all its burden was excess.
      doi: 10.1257/jep.29.1.163
    - id: lincoln
      title: The Window Tax
      authors: Lincoln Institute of Land Policy (Land Lines)
      year: 2015
      finding: 'Summary of Oates & Schwab: Ludlow window counts by tax notch and the excess-burden simulation.'
      url: https://www.lincolninst.edu/publications/articles/window-tax

    text taxcue "Taxed from 10" #b5482e at.data [2,10] font_size 30 hidden true
    text records "493 houses' tax records" at.data [1,22] cites ["oates_schwab"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text nopay "Pays no tax" at.data [1,22.5] font_size 30 hidden true
    text burden "Excess burden" #b5482e at.data [2.05,14] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Nine windows"

Why does a tax cost more than the money it raises? Start with the houses of Ludlow, a market town in England, around 1750.
    draw axes

B: Back then Britain taxed windows. From ten w (@draw taxcue@)indows up, a house paid tax by the window.

Two economists, Wallace Oates and Robert S (@draw records@)chwab, went through the old tax records for nearly five hundred Ludlow houses.

Abo (@draw bar_1@)ut four percent had eight windows. About five percent had t (@draw bar_3 ends@)en.

    erase records

    ask "Drag the nine-window bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "6" voice b
        intro: Your turn: what share do you think had exactly nine?
        right: Well judged: you said {g}, the records say {g.true} percent.
        wrong: You said {g}; the records say {g.true} percent.

Eighteen percent. Four times as many as either neighbour.
    highlight bar_2 effect box

B: Because nine was the last number that paid nothing. The same spikes sit just under the tax's higher steps, at fourteen and nineteen windows.
    highlight bar_2 effect pulse

    erase taxcue

So here's the puzzle. A nine-windo (@draw nopay@)w house pays no window tax at all.

B: Then for that family the tax costs nothing?
    highlight nopay

It does cost something: a dark room they'd have happily paid a window for. The state collects nothing, and the family is still worse off.
    highlight bar_2

That loss that nobody coll (@draw burden@)ects is the excess burden of a tax, or its deadweight loss.

    pause 0.5

## The missing loaves
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a week (Q)
    y_label: Price of a loaf (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 2
      side: seller
    units:
      price: [0, 4]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
    regions:
      - government_revenue
      - deadweight_loss
    readout:
      - revenue
      - dwl
    readout_at: inside

    card title "The missing loaves"

B: Windows are an odd case. Does an ordinary tax do the same? Take bread in Harbourtown, at Mia's bakery, with illustrative numbers.
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S

Without any tax (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar@), say five hundred loaves a week sell at two dollars each.

    ask "A $1 tax per loaf, paid by Mia. Move her supply curve, then press Done." on supply_curve predict true check size store t voice b
        intro: Now the harbour council wants a dollar on every loaf, collected from Mia. Where does her supply curve go?
        right: Yes. {t.why}
        wrong: Not quite. {t.why}

Straight up by (@animate tax.amount 25 duration 2.5@) the dollar: to bake the same loaf, Mia now needs a dollar more than before.
    draw tax_supply_curve label_S_tax

Buyers pay more, Mia keeps less, and fewer loaves are sold.
    draw tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps

B: And the council gets th (@draw wedge_region readout_revenue@)e dollar times every loaf still sold. That rectangle.

    hide label_wedge

That money isn't lost. It moves from buyers and Mia to the council, and pays for the harbour lights.
    highlight wedge_region

The loss is here: the loaves that are no longer sold.
    point at.ref tax_equilibrium_point gesture underline

Each of them w (@draw dwl_region readout_dwl@)as worth more to some buyer than it cost Mia to bake. Now nobody bakes it, nobody eats it, and nobody collects tax on it.

    hide label_DWL

B: Like the ninth window. A loaf that isn't sold pays no tax.
    highlight dwl_region effect pulse

Exactly. That triangle, about seventy dollars a week here, is what buyers and Mia lose on top of the council's three hundred and fifty. That's why a tax costs more than it raises.
    highlight readout effect box

    pause 0.5

## Double the tax
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Loaves a week (Q)
    y_label: Price of a loaf (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 25
      side: seller
    units:
      price: [0, 4]
      quantity: [0, 1000]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: loaves
    regions:
      - government_revenue
      - deadweight_loss
    readout:
      - revenue
      - dwl
    readout_at: inside

    scratch math top-right work [{"tex":"\\text{DWL} = \\tfrac12 \\times t \\times \\Delta Q"},{"tex":"\\Delta Q \\propto t"},{"tex":"\\text{DWL} \\propto t^2"}] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Double the tax"

So a dollar tax leaves a small triangle. What if the council doubles it to two dollars?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps

    draw wedge_region dwl_region readout

    hide label_wedge label_DWL

    ask "Double the tax: how many times bigger is the deadweight loss?" estimate.min 1 estimate.max 8 estimate.value 4 estimate.unit times store g stage own voice b
        intro: Guess first: how many times bigger does the waste get? My own guess would be twice.
        right: Yes: you said {g}, and it's about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's about {g.true}.

    erase estimate_1

Four times. Twice the tax makes the triangle twice as tall.
    animate tax.amount 50 duration 3 ghost true

    hide label_wedge label_DWL

And twice as wide, because twice as many loaves go unsold. Twice the height times twice the width: four times the area.
    highlight dwl_region effect pulse

B: And the revenue? Did that double too?
    highlight readout_revenue effect box

Less than double: the tax per loaf doubled, but fewer loaves are left to tax.
    highlight wedge_region

B: So the waste grows with the square of the tax, and the money doesn't keep up.
    highlight readout_dwl effect box

That's why economists like low rates on broad bases: many small triangles waste far less than one big one.
    animate tax.amount 25 duration 2

Try it: drag the taxed curve up, and watch the waste outrun the revenue.
    explore

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see why it squares?

@math

    hide mathq

The triangle's ar (@draw math_box math_line_1@)ea is half its height, the tax t, times its width, the lost loaves.

With straight curve (@draw math_line_2@)s, the lost loaves grow in step with the tax.

So the area g (@draw math_line_3@)rows with t times t: t squared.

    erase math

@after

    hide mathq

    pause 0.3

## Is every tax wasteful?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: The council should hire you before the next budget.
    good: You'd keep most of the triangles small.
    poor: Even Henry George needed a few tries.
    none: Taxes are slippery; the triangle is worth another look.
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Quantity (Q)
    y_label: Price (P)
    demand:
      elasticity: 1
    supply:
      elasticity: 1
    equilibrium:
      show: true
      guides: true
    tax:
      amount: 25
      side: seller
    regions:
      - government_revenue
      - deadweight_loss
sources:
    - id: george1879
      title: Progress and Poverty
      authors: George, H.
      year: 1879
      finding: Argued for a single tax on the value of land, which cannot be reduced in supply by taxing it.
    - id: feldstein1999
      title: Tax Avoidance and the Deadweight Loss of the Income Tax
      authors: Feldstein, M.
      year: 1999
      finding: Counting all the ways taxable income responds, the deadweight loss of the US personal income tax may be as much as 30% of revenue, more than ten times Harberger's classic estimate.
      doi: 10.1162/003465399558391
    - id: ssg2012
      title: 'The Elasticity of Taxable Income with Respect to Marginal Tax Rates: A Critical Review'
      authors: Saez, E., Slemrod, J. & Giertz, S. H.
      year: 2012
      finding: The best estimates of the elasticity of taxable income are well below those Feldstein used, implying a smaller deadweight loss.
    - id: r109
      title: 'Rundskriv R-109/14: Prinsipper og krav ved utarbeidelse av samfunnsøkonomiske analyser mv.'
      authors: Finansdepartementet (Norway)
      year: 2014
      finding: 'For measures financed over public budgets, a tax-financing cost is included: ''Skattekostnaden settes til 20 øre per krone.'' (also in R-14/99 and R-109/2005)'
      url: https://www.regjeringen.no/globalassets/upload/fin/vedlegg/okstyring/rundskriv/faste/r_109_2014.pdf

    portrait george cites ["george1879"] of "Henry George" cameo true hidden true
    scratch evid top-right work ["US income tax, 1999: up to 30¢ per $","later studies: less","Norway: +20 øre per krone"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch sure top-right work ["Waste grows with the tax: consensus","How big: contested"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Is every tax wasteful?"

B: Is every tax this wasteful, though?
    draw axes demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Pstar label_Qstar tax_supply_curve label_S_tax tax_equilibrium_point tax_guide_lines price_buyers_point label_Pb price_sellers_point label_Ps

    draw wedge_region dwl_region

    hide label_wedge label_DWL

Not every one. The triangle comes from loaves that stop being made. So a tax on something whose amount can't shrink...
    highlight dwl_region

B: Like land. Nobody makes less land because it's taxed. Henr (@draw george@)y George's 1879 book built a whole movement on that idea.

    erase george

Then the supply curve stands upright. The tax can't change the quantity, and the triangle shrinks to nothing: the owners pay it all.
    animate supply.elasticity 0.06 duration 3 ghost true

    hide label_wedge label_DWL

B: And for real taxes, on work and on spending? How big is the waste?
    animate supply.elasticity 1 duration 2

    hide label_wedge label_DWL

There we disagree. In 1999 Martin Feldstein put th (@draw evid_box evid_line_1@)e waste of the US income tax at as much as thirty cents per dollar raised.

B: Later studies found peop (@draw evid_line_2@)le respond less than he assumed, so the waste is smaller.

B: Norway's finance ministry tel (@draw evid_line_3@)ls planners to count twenty øre of extra cost for every krone of tax.

    erase evid

That a tax costs more than (@draw sure_box sure_line_1@) it raises, and the more so the bigger it gets, is consensus.

Ho (@draw sure_line_2@)w much is contested.

    erase sure

    quiz "A city doubles its hotel tax from $5 to $10 a night. Roughly what happens to the deadweight loss?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: Roughly four times: the triangle grows twice as tall and about twice as wide.
        wrong: Both the height and the width of the triangle grow.
        * It doubles
        + It roughly quadruples
        * It stays the same
        * It halves

    quiz "Which tax wastes least?"
        right: Land: its amount can't shrink when it is taxed, so no trades are lost.
        wrong: Which of these can't shrink when it is taxed?
        * A tax on one brand of cola
        + A tax on land
        * A tax on restaurant meals

    quiz "A country taxes imported bananas so heavily that nobody imports any. The tax raises nothing. What does it cost?" voice b
        right: The lost bananas: all of it is deadweight loss, and none of it is revenue.
        wrong: Count what buyers and sellers lose, not what the state gets.
        * Nothing, since nothing is collected
        + The bananas people no longer get to eat
        * Exactly what it would have raised

So what does a tax destroy? Not the money it raises; that only changes hands. It destroys the trades that no longer happen, like the ninth window, and the loss grows with the square of the tax.
    highlight dwl_region effect pulse

    pause 0.6

